[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation uses [38;5;10mextern "C"[0m FFI to call OpenSSL functions ([38;5;10mEVP_CIPHER_CTX_new[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_CIPHER_CTX_free[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_aes_256_ecb[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_EncryptInit_ex[0m, [38;5;10mEVP_EncryptUpdate[0m) for the AES-256-ECB operations needed by the NIST DRBG (random number generator). These are used by functions like [38;5;10maes256_ecb[0m, [38;5;10mrandombytes_drbg[0m, etc.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
When the shared library ([38;5;10mlibsha2.so[0m) was loaded at runtime, the dynamic linker couldn't resolve these OpenSSL symbols because the library was never told to link against [38;5;10mlibcrypto[0m. This caused a [38;5;10mDlOpen[0m error:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mundefined symbol: EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The original C code links against [38;5;10mlibcrypto[0m via CMake ([38;5;10mtarget_link_libraries[0m), but the Rust translation's [38;5;10mCargo.toml[0m had no equivalent link directive.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## What I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Added a single file — [38;5;10mtranslated_rust/build.rs[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10mfn main() {
    println!("cargo:rustc-link-lib=crypto");
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This tells the Rust linker to link the [38;5;10mcdylib[0m against [38;5;10mlibcrypto.so[0m, which provides the OpenSSL symbols. The Rust code itself (SHA-256 implementation, [38;5;10mseed_state[0m, etc.) was correct — it was purely a linking issue.[0m[0m
